    {
      "id": "word-tratabas--try",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tratabas",
      "answerEn": "you were trying",
      "noteEn": "Tratabas is a past form of tratar for one familiar person. With de before an action, tratar means “to try”; the -abas ending presents that attempt as ongoing or as background rather than as one completed event.",
      "example": { "es": "Tratabas de abrir la puerta.", "en": "You were trying to open the door." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "imperfect", "informal singular"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-abriera--open-up-subjunctive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "abriera",
      "answerEn": "I opened up; I would open up (a special past form)",
      "noteEn": "Abriera is a past subjunctive form of abrir. The subjunctive is a special verb form used when an action is wanted, attempted, doubted, or otherwise not presented as a plain fact. With me and a person introduced by a, abrirse means to open up emotionally.",
      "example": { "es": "Ella quería que yo me abriera más.", "en": "She wanted me to open up more." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "subjunctive", "opening up"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--abrirse-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "to; introduces the person someone opens up to",
      "noteEn": "The expression abrirse a alguien uses a before the person who receives the honest or personal disclosure.",
      "example": { "es": "Me abrí a una amiga.", "en": "I opened up to a friend." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "verb complement", "opening up"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-herir",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "herir",
      "answerEn": "to hurt; to wound",
      "noteEn": "Herir can describe a physical wound or emotional hurt, especially with sentimientos (“feelings”).",
      "example": { "es": "No quería herir sus sentimientos.", "en": "I did not want to hurt her feelings." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "harm"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-sentimientos",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "sentimientos",
      "answerEn": "feelings; emotions",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "emotion", "plural noun"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-daño--harm",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "daño",
      "answerEn": "harm; damage",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "harm", "noun"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--harm-target",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; introduces the person or group harmed",
      "noteEn": "After hacer daño, a introduces the affected person, animal, group, or thing.",
      "example": { "es": "El ruido hace daño a los animales.", "en": "The noise harms animals." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "harm target"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-hacerte--harm",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "hacerte",
      "answerEn": "to hurt you; to cause you harm (with daño)",
      "noteEn": "Hacerte joins the unchanged verb hacer to te, which identifies one familiar person as affected. In hacerte daño, daño supplies the harm and the complete expression means to hurt you.",
      "example": { "es": "No quiero hacerte daño.", "en": "I do not want to hurt you." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "attached pronoun", "harm"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-del--comparison-clause",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "del",
      "answerEn": "than what; combines de + el in an amount comparison",
      "noteEn": "Before que and a complete second action, del can refer back to a masculine singular amount or thing being compared. El matches that earlier noun, as in más tiempo del que necesitamos (“more time than we need”).",
      "example": { "es": "Compró más pan del que podía comer.", "en": "He bought more bread than he could eat." },
      "tags": ["word", "comparison", "contraction", "relative clause"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--after-del-comparison",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; completes del que in an amount comparison",
      "noteEn": "In más + noun + del que, que connects the amount named before del with the full action used as the other side of the comparison.",
      "example": { "es": "Hay más trabajo del que pensábamos.", "en": "There is more work than we thought." },
      "tags": ["word", "comparison", "relative connector"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-podrías--conditional",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "podrías",
      "answerEn": "you could; you would be able to",
      "noteEn": "Podrías is the conditional, or “would,” form of poder for one familiar person. Poder changes to the irregular stem podr- and adds -ías; the following action stays in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "example": { "es": "Podrías venir mañana.", "en": "You could come tomorrow." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "conditional", "informal singular"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-hacerme--harm",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "hacerme",
      "answerEn": "to hurt me; to do harm to me (with daño supplied by context)",
      "noteEn": "Hacerme joins the unchanged verb hacer to me, which marks the speaker as affected. When daño has already been named, hacerme can carry the sense of doing that harm to me without repeating daño.",
      "example": { "es": "No podrías hacerme tanto daño.", "en": "You could not hurt me that much." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "attached pronoun", "harm"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-nunca--ever-in-comparison",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "nunca",
      "answerEn": "ever (inside a hypothetical comparison)",
      "noteEn": "Nunca usually means “never.” Inside a clause about what someone could possibly do, natural English can use “ever” because the negative or limiting force comes from the wider comparison.",
      "example": { "es": "Es más de lo que podría gastar nunca.", "en": "It is more than I could ever spend." },
      "tags": ["word", "negative word", "comparison", "contextual sense"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a--emphatic-object-pronoun",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; introduces an emphasized object such as a mí",
      "noteEn": "A stressed form such as a mí can repeat a short object pronoun such as me for contrast or emphasis. English normally expresses the emphasis through stress rather than an extra word.",
      "example": { "es": "No puedes verme a mí.", "en": "You cannot see me in particular." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "object pronoun", "emphasis"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-penetrado--entered",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "penetrado",
      "answerEn": "penetrated; gotten inside",
      "noteEn": "Penetrado is penetrar's completed-action form. It can describe entering something physically or, figuratively, reaching beneath the surface of a person's thoughts or feelings.",
      "example": { "es": "La luz ha penetrado en la habitación.", "en": "The light has entered the room." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "participle", "figurative sense"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-en--penetrar-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "en",
      "answerEn": "into; introduces what is entered after penetrar",
      "noteEn": "Penetrar en presents a movement or reach into a place, material, subject, or person's inner world.",
      "example": { "es": "El agua penetró en la pared.", "en": "The water seeped into the wall." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "verb complement"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-interior--inner-self",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "interior",
      "answerEn": "inner self; interior",
      "noteEn": "As a noun, interior can mean the inside of something. Applied to a person, it can refer figuratively to private thoughts, feelings, or character.",
      "example": { "es": "Guardaba ese miedo en su interior.", "en": "She kept that fear inside herself." },
      "tags": ["word", "noun", "figurative sense", "inner self"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-conocerás--meet-future",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "conocerás",
      "answerEn": "you will meet; you will get to know",
      "noteEn": "Conocerás is the future form of conocer for one familiar person. With people not yet known, conocer often means to meet or become acquainted with them, not merely to know a fact.",
      "example": { "es": "Conocerás a mis amigos mañana.", "en": "You will meet my friends tomorrow." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "future", "meeting people"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-verás--see-future",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "verás",
      "answerEn": "you will see",
      "noteEn": "Verás keeps the whole verb ver and adds -ás, the future ending for one familiar person.",
      "example": { "es": "Verás el resultado mañana.", "en": "You will see the result tomorrow." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "future", "informal singular"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-tratar-de-que-subjunctive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "tratar de que + otra persona + subjuntivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone tries to get a different person to do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of tratar, then de que, then identify the other person and put that person's action in a special subjunctive form.",
      "noteEn": "For the same person's action, use tratar de plus the verb's unchanged dictionary form: trato de salir (“I try to leave”). When the intended action belongs to someone else, use de que and the subjunctive, a special form for an intended action not stated as fact: trato de que Marta salga. After a past form of tratar, that action often uses a past subjunctive form such as saliera.",
      "example": { "es": "Tratamos de que Ana llegue temprano.", "en": "We try to get Ana to arrive early." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "attempt", "subjunctive", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-abrirse-a-alguien",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "abrirse a alguien",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone opens up and shares personal feelings with another person?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of abrirse, followed by a and the person they open up to.",
      "noteEn": "Abrirse literally suggests opening oneself, but with a person it means becoming emotionally honest or sharing private thoughts. The small pronoun changes with the person opening up: me abro, te abres, se abre, nos abrimos, and so on. The a before the listener stays.",
      "example": { "es": "Luis se abrió a su amiga.", "en": "Luis opened up to his friend." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "communication", "feelings", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-hacer-daño-a",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "hacer daño (a alguien o algo)",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that an action hurts or harms someone or something?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of hacer with daño. Introduce a named affected person or thing with a, or mark the affected person with a small pronoun such as me, te, or le.",
      "noteEn": "Hacer daño works as a unit for causing physical or emotional harm. The affected person can appear after a, as in hace daño a Pablo, or in a small pronoun: me hizo daño (“it hurt me”) and hacerte daño (“to hurt you”). Hacer changes for person and time; daño stays unchanged.",
      "example": { "es": "Ese comentario le hizo daño a Pablo.", "en": "That comment hurt Pablo." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "collocation", "harm", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-mejor-que",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "mejor que",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone does something better than someone else?",
      "answerEn": "Use mejor for “better,” then que before the person or thing being compared.",
      "noteEn": "Mejor already contains the idea of “more good” or “better,” so do not add más before it. When mejor describes how an action is done, it does not change for gender or number. Que introduces the other side of the comparison.",
      "example": { "es": "Ana cocina mejor que yo.", "en": "Ana cooks better than I do." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "comparison", "irregular comparative", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-mas-noun-del-que",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "más + nombre + del/de la/de los/de las que + verbo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say “more of something than someone needs, uses, or can provide”?",
      "answerEn": "Put más before the naming word, then use del que, de la que, de los que, or de las que before the second action. Match el, la, los, or las to that naming word.",
      "noteEn": "A naming word is a noun such as tiempo, agua, libros, or horas. The article in the comparison repeats its gender and number: más tiempo del que, más agua de la que, más libros de los que, and más horas de las que. Del combines de and el. The following verb states the amount on the other side of the comparison.",
      "example": { "es": "Tenemos más tiempo del que necesitamos.", "en": "We have more time than we need." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "comparison", "quantity", "B1"]
    }
  ],
